11
00:46:57,692 --> 00:47:03,692
8 Mile is a 2002 hip-hop biographical drama film produced and directed
by Curtis Hanson from a script written by Scott Silver.

12
00:51:13,846 --> 00:51:19,846
It stars Eminem in his film debut, alongside Mekhi Phifer, Brittany Murphy, Michael
Shannon, Kim Basinger and Anthony Mackie, the latter also in his film debut.

13
00:55:29,999 --> 00:55:35,999
The film, which contains autobiographical elements from
Eminem's life, follows Detroit rapper Jimmy Smith Jr.

14
00:59:46,153 --> 00:59:52,153
aka B-Rabbit (Eminem) and his attempt to launch a career in
hip-hop, a music genre dominated by African Americans.

15
01:04:02,307 --> 01:04:08,307
The title is derived from 8 Mile Road, the road between the predominantly black city of Detroit and
the largely white suburban communities to the north that Eminem originally lived in.

16
01:08:18,461 --> 01:08:24,461
8 Mile was released by Universal Pictures on November 8, 2002 in
the United States and January 2, 2003 in Germany.

17
01:12:34,615 --> 01:12:40,615
The film was a critical and commercial success.

18
01:16:50,769 --> 01:16:56,769
It opened at No.

19
01:21:06,923 --> 01:21:12,923
1 in the US with $51.3 million grossed in its opening weekend and
an eventual total of $242.9 million worldwide.

20
01:25:23,076 --> 01:25:29,076
The film's accompanying soundtrack (released by Universal's then subsidiary Universal Music, through Interscope and Shady/Aftermath) was
also a commercial success, being certified quadruple platinum by the Recording Industry Association of America (RIAA).

21
01:29:39,230 --> 01:29:45,230
The film received generally positive reviews, praising the music and
performances, though some noted its unoriginality.

22
01:33:55,384 --> 01:34:01,384
The film garnered numerous award nominations and wins, including an Academy Award for Best Original Song
win for "Lose Yourself" for Eminem, Jeff Bass and Luis Resto at the 75th Academy Awards.

23
01:38:11,538 --> 01:38:17,538
8 Mile was named one of the best films of 2002 by several publications.

24
01:42:27,692 --> 01:42:33,692
The film explores themes of individual, adolescence,
rap music, hip-hop, rhyme battle.

25
01:46:43,846 --> 01:46:49,846
The tone is emotional.
